# A Versatile Prodrug Strategy to In Situ Encapsulate Drugs in MOF Nanocarriers: A Case of Cytarabine-IR820 Prodrug Encapsulated ZIF-8 toward Chemo-Photothermal Therapy

## abstract
Abstract: Zeolitic imidazolate framework‐8 (ZIF‐8) is an attractive metal organic framework (MOF) in drug delivery. Strong interaction between drugs and ZIF‐8 is essential for high drug loadings through in situ construction of MOFs. However, only limited drugs with unique functional groups (COOH, SO3H, et al.) can interact with ZIF‐8 and be encapsulated satisfactorily so far. Drugs without these functional groups are difficult to be loaded due to the lack of strong interaction. Herein a versatile prodrug strategy is proposed to solve the problems encountered by MOFs. Cytarabine (Ara) is chosen as a model drug since it cannot be loaded in ZIF‐8 satisfactorily by itself. New indocyanine green (IR820) is utilized to bond with Ara for the formation of prodrug (Ara‐IR820) and endows the prodrug with fluorescence imaging‐guided chemo‐photothermal therapy, in which sulfonic groups strengthen the interaction between prodrug and ZIF‐8. This prodrug loaded ZIF‐8 is further functionalized with hyaluronic acid (HA) to result in active‐targeting HA/Ara‐IR820@ZIF‐8 nanoparticles. The in vitro and in vivo results demonstrate its excellent visual cancer therapy with tumor‐targeted and pH‐responsive release behavior. This design offers a new concept to solve the drug loading problem of MOFs, exhibiting a flexible strategy to expand the biomedical applications of MOFs.

Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) is an attractive metal organic framework (MOF) in drug delivery. Strong interaction between drugs and ZIF-8 is essential for high drug loadings through in situ construction of MOFs. However, only limited drugs with unique functional groups (COOH, $-s\mathsf{o}_{3}\mathsf{H}$ , et al.) can interact with ZIF-8 and be encapsulated satisfactorily so far. Drugs without these functional groups are difficult to be loaded due to the lack of strong interaction. Herein a versatile prodrug strategy is proposed to solve the problems encountered by MOFs. Cytarabine (Ara) is chosen as a model drug since it cannot be loaded in ZIF-8 satisfactorily by itself. New indocyanine green (IR820) is utilized to bond with Ara for the formation of prodrug (Ara-IR820) and endows the prodrug with fluorescence imagingguided chemo-photothermal therapy, in which sulfonic groups strengthen the interaction between prodrug and ZIF-8. This prodrug loaded ZIF-8 is further functionalized with hyaluronic acid (HA) to result in active-targeting HA/ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 nanoparticles. The in vitro and in vivo results demonstrate its excellent visual cancer therapy with tumor-targeted and pH-responsive release behavior. This design offers a new concept to solve the drug loading problem of MOFs, exhibiting a flexible strategy to expand the biomedical applications of MOFs.
## 1. Introduction
Cancer has become a more and more serious problem for human health with the deterioration of environment and the change of people lifestyle.[1] Chemotherapy was the most popular treatment for cancers because it exhibited high systemic therapeutic efficacy and good patient compliance through easy operation.[2] Nanoscale drug delivery system was reported to improve the curative effects of chemotherapeutic drugs by improving its biodistribution and preventing biodegradation in systemic circulation as a result of the enhanced permeability and retention (EPR) effect.[3] In recent years, metal organic framework materials (MOFs) with huge porosity, tunable structure, and ease of modification have been regarded as ideal drug nanocarriers.[4]
In general, two approaches have been widely applied to realize the drug delivery based on MOFs.[5] 1) Post absorbing drugs after the synthesis of MOFs: this method was suitable for drugs with smaller size than that of the pores in MOFs, which would cause drug leakage during the cleaning process, resulting in a low drug loading content and burst release behavior. 2) One-step in situ encapsulation of drugs during the construction of MOFs: this way could realize the loading of drugs with smaller/larger structures via the generation of flaws in MOFs, avoiding drug leakage before the degradation of carriers; however, strong interaction between drugs and MOFs was essential for this case to realize satisfactory drug loading and drug release behavior. Until now, only limited molecules (camptothecin,[6] doxorubicin,[7] caffeine,[5] etc.) with special functional groups $_{(-\mathrm{COOH}}$ , $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , and so on) or opposite charge have been successfully confined within MOFs through in situ encapsulation by their strong interactions with MOFs. For the common drugs without strong interactions with MOFs, it was highly demanded to realize an efficient strategy for solving their low drug loading content and burst release behavior in MOF carriers. Among MOFs, zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) was the most attractive drug carrier because of its good biocompatibility and pH-responsive degradability for controlled drug release.[8] Consequently, it was of great significance to make the ZIF-8 as a versatile drug carrier platform with successful drug encapsulation for various common drugs.
New indocyanine green (IR820) was a derivative of indocyanine green, which was the only approved near infrared dye for clinical application by the United States Food and Drug Administration.[9] IR820 was able to produce heat under the irradiation of near infrared light for photothermal therapy (PTT). Moreover, it could also be used for fluorescence imaging to realize the diagnosis of cancers.[10] Therefore, it was an attractive therapeutic agent used for construction of multifunctional therapy platform.
Herein, we proposed a robust prodrug strategy for encapsulating common drugs in ZIF-8 based on the flexible chemistry modification. This concept could solve the drug loading problems encountered by MOFs. Cytarabine (Ara) was chosen as the model drug, since it failed to be loaded into ZIF-8 by itself because of the leakage resulted from its small molecular structure and weak interaction with ZIF-8. IR820 was utilized to bond with Ara for the formation of prodrug (Ara-IR820) because its sulfonic group could coordinate with the metal ions in ZIF-8,[11] strengthening the interactions and accomplishing a high drug loading. The hyaluronic acid (HA) was further functionalized on the Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 hybrids via the coordination effect between its carboxyls and metal ions in ZIF-8 to realize the tumor-targeted delivery system $(\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara-IR}820@\$ ZIF-8).[12,13] The fabrication of our drug delivery system was through in situ encapsulation, as illustrated in Scheme 1. This newly designed HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 drug delivery system had the following advantages: 1) The introduction of sulfonic group increased the interaction between drugs and MOFs, achieving high drug loading content of Ara in ZIF-8; 2) The photothermal agent and chemotherapeutic drug coloaded
MOF was multifunctional, performing the combination effect of fluorescence imaging-guided PTT and chemotherapy on cancers; 3) The construction of prodrug could prevent the degradation of amino in Ara by deaminase during systemic circulation;[14] 4) The EPR effect, active-targeting, and pH-responsive degradation of carriers made the prodrug release selectively/ controllably at tumor regions. After that, the amide linkages in prodrugs would be hydrolyzed by the amidase overexpressed in lysosomes of cancer cells to release parent Ara.[15,16] We anticipate that this work will result in an efficient strategy to solve the drug loading problems encountered by MOFs, realizing an excellent tumor-targeted and fluorescence imaging-guided chemo-photothermal therapy.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Ara-IR820 prodrug was designed to be loaded into ZIF-8 during the in situ formation of MOF by an environmentally friendly process. Ara alone failed to be loaded into ZIF-8 because of its small molecular structure (easy for leakage) without strong interaction with MOF. However, the loading content for free IR820 was tested to be high because of its proper molecular structure and the high coordination interaction between sulfonate with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ . In our design, the chemotherapeutic drug Ara was dragged into ZIF-8 by the IR820 based on the formation of Ara-IR820 conjugate. HA was further introduced to modify Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 for resulting in HA/Ara- $\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8 nanoparticles. HA contained a lot of carboxyl groups which could interact strongly with ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ of ZIF-8.[17,18] Therefore, it could result in sufficient modification of HA on the Ara$\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ nanoparticles.
IR820 first reacted with 6-aminocaproic acid to introduce a carboxyl group for further modification (Figure S1, Supporting Information). The successful synthesis of IR820-COOH could be identified easily by the color change of reaction solution from green to blue. Pure IR820-COOH was prepared by the silica gel column chromatography and yield of this step was $48.8\%$ . $^1\mathrm{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer $({}^{1}\mathrm{H}~\mathrm{NMR})$ spectrum of IR820-COOH was displayed as the Figure 1A. The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR data could be completely interpreted as the designed structure. We could also find the $[\mathsf{M}+\mathsf{H}]^{+}$ ion at $m/z$ $:944.3986$ for IR820-COOH in electrospray ionization mass spectroscopy (ESI-MS) result (Figure S2A, Supporting Information).[19]
Then, carboxyl group of IR820-COOH was used to react with Ara via the typical amide reaction to prepare the Ara-IR820 prodrug (Figure S1, Supporting Information). Ara-IR820 was purified using silica gel column chromatography and the yield of Ara-IR820 was $61.2\%$ . Figure 1B displayed the $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of Ara-IR820, which proved that the molar ratio of IR820 and Ara was 1:1. The appearance of characteristic peak at $10.83~\mathrm{ppm}$ confirmed the successful amide reaction, which was attributed to the hydrogen in amide. Figure S2B (Supporting Information) showed the ESI-MS result of Ara-IR820 with the specific $[\mathrm{M}+\mathrm{H}]^{+}$ ion $(m/z=1169.4721_{.}$ ).
The successful construction of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was first verified by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD), as shown in Figure  S3 (Supporting Information). The  crystal structure of our prepared ZIF-8 was the same as that had been reported.[20] Therefore, the loading of prodrug did not break the structural integrity significantly. However, the diffraction peak of XRD for HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was masked to some extent because of the coating of HA, which could also confirm the successful cladding of HA.
Both Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 and HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 could be dispersed in water readily and no precipitation would appear after the storage at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for more than one month. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images displayed the morphologies of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 (Figure S4A, Supporting Information) and ${\mathrm{HA}}/{\mathrm{Ara}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{IR}}820@{\mathrm{ZIF}}{\mathrm{-}}8$ (Figure 2A). Both Ara$\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ and $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara}{\cdot}\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ were quasispherical nanoparticles. Particle size was an important characteristic of formulations to judge the feasibility for intravenous injection and the possibility for passive targeting drug delivery to tumor tissues. The particle size of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 determined by dynamic light scattering (DLS) was $120.5\pm1.8$ nm with the polydispersity index (PDI) of 0.005 in Figure S4B (Supporting Information), while $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\Sigma\mathrm{IF}.8$ exhibited a particle size of $132.0\pm9.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ $(\mathrm{PDI}=0.086)$ in Figure 2B. The hydrodynamic diameter of $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\mathrm{ZIF-}8$ measured by DLS was slightly larger than that calculated from TEM images $(117.4\pm6.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), which was due to the existence of hydration layer on the outer side of the particles in aqueous solution and the shrinkage of nanoparticles in a drying state during TEM measurements.[21,22] The good dispersibility confirmed by the appropriate size supplied by DLS was helpful for HA/ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 to be used as an intravenous formulation meeting the EPR effect.
Then, we studied the temperature-rising ability of HA/AraIR820@ZIF-8 for PTT in vitro. Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820, IR820, ZIF-8, and water were tested together for comparison, and the results were shown in Figure 2C,D. For formulations with the same concentration of IR820 $(118\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ ), the increased temperature was 26.7, 26.9, 16.0, and $21.8^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $\mathrm{HA}/$ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820, and IR820, respectively, after the irradiation of 5 min. It was obvious that the photothermal conversion efficiency of Ara-IR820 was lower than that of IR820. However, the encapsulation of ZIF-8 on the outside of Ara-IR820 prodrug could increase the photothermal conversion efficacy to a great extent. We attributed this improvement to the increase of photostability for Ara-IR820 and the thermal storage capacity from ZIF-8. The increased temperature of ZIF-8 and water groups could be ignored. The temperature-rising effects of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 with varying concentrations were further studied. As shown in Figure  2E, the temperature increased 26.7, 16.3, 8.8, 7.2, and $2.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara}.\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}.8$ with the concentration ranging from 118 to $0\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ , demonstrating the concentration dependent characteristic. Therefore, the wonderful temperature-rising performance of HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 made it have the potential to be used for PTT.
Drug loading content for Ara-IR820 prodrug was measured utilizing a UV–vis spectrophotometer after the decomposing of ZIF-8 in strong acidic environment, which was ${\approx}39.8\%$ . In the acidic condition, the 2-methylimidazole linkers were protonated, which broke the coordination between zinc ions and imidazolate rings.[23] This made ZIF-8 as a promising pHresponsive carrier. The pH-responsive release was verified via the in vitro release experiment. The pH value of normal physiological environment was generally 7.4,[24] while the $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ value decreased at the tumor area because of the abnormal proliferation. In this situation, the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of endosomes and lysosomes in cancer cells would be about 4.5–6.5.[25] As a result, the phosphate buffer saline (PBS, $\mathrm{pH}=7.4)$ was chosen to simulate the normal tissue fluid, and the PBS $\mathrm{(pH=}5.0\$ ) was designed to simulate the intracellular fluid of cancer cells. As shown in Figure S5 (Supporting Information), the release rate was higher for $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara}.\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}.8$ dispersed in the PBS $(\mathrm{pH}=5.0)$ ) than that in PBS with the pH of 7.4. The accumulative release amount of $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\mathrm{ZIF-}8$ reached $38\%$ in PBS $\mathrm{(pH}=5.0\$ ) in the first $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . During the same time, $\mathrm{HA}/$ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 dispersed in PBS $\mathrm{(pH=7.4)}$ ) only released $8.2\%$ . At the end of the experiment, the HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 in acidic environment released $99.1\%$ of Ara-IR820 in total after the incubation of $36\mathrm{h}$ . However, the accumulative release amount $(52.2\%)$ in neutral PBS was much lower than that. This $\mathrm{\pH}$ -responsive drug release behavior made it possible for HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 to be used for the appointed release in tumor cells with lower $\mathrm{\pH}$ value, avoiding the adverse reactions of chemotherapeutic drugs in normal tissues and achieving an ideal treatment effect for cancers.
The ability to get into cell cytosol played a key role for cytotoxicity of formulations. Murine mammary carcinoma cells (4T1 cells) were chosen as a model to evaluate the cellular uptake behavior for HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 because of its high expression of HA receptor (CD44).[26] Fluorescent images (Figure 3A) taken by a fluorescent inverted microscope exhibited the intracellular fluorescent accumulation after the treatment with PBS, free IR820, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 only and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 added with pretreatment of HA. It was evident that the fluorescent intensity was higher in cells treated with Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 than that treated with free IR820 due to the different approaches to enter cells. IR820 was taken in cells via the passive diffusion,[27] while it had been reported that nanoparticles could be absorbed by cells through endocytosis. This endocytosis approach could avoid the efflux of drugs mediated by p-glycoprotein, solving the multidrug resistance (MDR) effect.[28] Cells treated with HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 exhibited further higher fluorescent intensity than that treated with nontargeted Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 because endocytosis mediated by HA receptors was much efficient and fast. To demonstrate the effect of HA receptor clearly, competitive inhibition experiment for CD44 was carried out. 4T1 cells were incubated with free HA for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to occupy CD44 before the addition of $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\Sigma\mathrm{IF}.8$ , inhibiting the CD44 mediated active targeting uptake. The resultant intracellular fluorescence intensity was lower than that of HA/ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group, which was similar to nontargeted Ara-IR820@ZIF-8. The results of following flow cytometry (Figure 3B) were in agreement with the qualitative experiment. The cellular uptake for IR820 was limited by the pathway to enter cells and the MDR effect. This cellular uptake for HA/ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was the highest thanks to the CD44 mediated endocytosis, indicating its great potential for increased cell inhibition rates.
The antitumor ability of ${\mathrm{HA}}/{\mathrm{Ara}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{IR}}820@{\mathrm{ZIF}}{\mathrm{-}}8$ with combined chemotherapy and PTT by laser irradiation was studied in vitro. Cell inhibition rates of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 $^+$ laser, AraIR820 prodrug $^+$ laser, physical mixture of Ara and IR820 with the molar ratio of 1:1 $(\mathtt{A r a}+\mathtt{I R820})$ 1 $^+$ laser, $\mathrm{IR}820+$ laser, Ara $^+$ laser, and laser only were also tested for comparison. Figure 3C showed that the cell inhibition rates of both IR820 and Ara with laser irradiation were much lower than that of Ara- $\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\mathrm{ZIF-}8$ groups with laser. The cell inhibition rates of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 and HA/Ara- $\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8 could reach above $90\%$ at a relatively low concentration $(10\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M})$ , demonstrating their excellent in vitro antitumor ability through combined chemo-photothermal therapy. Moreover, $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\Sigma\mathrm{IF}.8$ exhibited slightly higher cell inhibition rate than Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 due to its enhanced cellular uptake. The half-maximum inhibitory concentration ( $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ value) of each formulation was further calculated according to the cell inhibition rates at different concentrations. The results were listed in Table 1. The $\mathrm{IC}_{50}$ value of our as-prepared $\mathrm{HA}/$ A $\mathtt{i r a-I R820@Z}$ IF-8 was $2.02\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ , which was 1.46 times lower than that of Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8, 5.45 times lower than that of IR820, and 167.24 times lower than that of Ara. These results indicated that $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara-IR8}20@\Sigma\mathrm{IF}.8$ with laser could kill cancer cells at a very low dose, further demonstrating its powerful antitumor activity by combined chemo-photothermal therapy.
Before the animal experiment in vivo, it was essential to study the safety of formulations for intravenous injection. Hemolytic toxicity was thus evaluated for $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara–IR}820@\$ ZIF-8 with the concentrations varying from 10 to $500\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . As shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information), the hemoly­sis ratio of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was below $5\%$ even at the highest concentration, indicating negligible red blood cells would be destroyed by this formulation. It had been reported that the hemolysis ratio below $10\%$ could be acceptable in intravenous administration.[29] Therefore, the low hemolytic toxicity of HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 demonstrated that our formulation was suitable for intravenous administration.
Fluorescence imaging was an important diagnostic method for the real-time tracking of formulations. Here, IR820 component in HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 could be employed to monitor the biodistribution of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 in vivo by taking fluorescence imaging. This fluorescence imaging was important for determining the best time to give laser, resulting in fluorescence imaging-guided therapy. The in vivo drug distributions after the injection of IR820, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, as well as HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 were displayed in Figure 4A. For comparison, the drug distribution in dissected organs (heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney) and tumors was also studied, as shown in Figure 4B. In mice treated with free IR820, IR820 was distributed throughout the body during the monitoring, especially at the chest. At the end of this experiment, mice were executed and dissected to observe the fluorescence in organs, indicating that most of IR820 were distributed in liver and the fluorescence intensity in tumors was extremely low. IR820 was mainly transferred to liver because it was the major metabolic organ.[30] Fluorescence intensity of Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 at tumor site was stronger than that of free IR820 because of the EPR effect, reflecting passive tumor-targeted delivery. The biodistribution of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was compared with that of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 to verify the HA mediated active targeting effect. As displayed in Figure 4A, HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 could accumulate at the tumor area in $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , which was more quick than Ara-IR820@ZIF-8. After that, HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 kept high concentration at tumors during the test of $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, isolated tumor still contained the most amount of HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8, which was much higher than that of other groups. This real-time monitoring for Ara- $\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8 and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 showed that the fluorescence intensity in tumors was the strongest at $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ after the injection. Therefore, we decided to provide the laser irradiation at that time for the in vivo antitumor therapy in the subsequent experiment. The in vivo near-infrared fluorescence (NIRF) imaging results not only confirmed the excellent tumor-targeted drug delivery of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, but also realized a fluorescence imaging-guided therapy.
To study the in vivo photothermal abilities of HA/AraIR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, and IR820, we performed the test upon the 4T1 bearing mice using normal saline as control. Mice were irradiated with laser at tumor regions in $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ after the injection of different formulations. As shown in Figure 5A, the temperature of tumors rose quickly at the first 2 min and then turned to  slow increase. Temperature reached 40.0, 46.1, and $48.5~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for IR820 group, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group, and HA/ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group, respectively, after the irradiation of 2 min. Based on the in vivo infrared thermography images in Figure 5B, the final temperature for IR820 group, Ara- $\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8 group, and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group was 41.8, 48.6, and $50.0^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $5\mathrm{min}$ . The temperature-rising effect of IR820 in vivo was significantly lower than that in vitro because of its poor photostability, high degradability, and nontargeted distribution in physiological conditions. The package of ZIF-8 on the outside of Ara-IR820 could protect the prodrug from degradation in body with passive and active targeting drug delivery, and therefore Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group showed higher temperatures. Based on the higher photothermal abilities, the heat generated by Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was sufficient to induce the cell death in tumors for the PTT.
The above experimental results proved that HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 performed excellent pH-responsive release behavior, high cell inhibition rates toward 4T1 cells, low hemolytic toxicity, satisfactory ability of active targeting drug delivery, and effective heat-generation capacity. Therefore, in vivo antitumor efficacy study was performed on the 4T1-bearing mice with laser irradiation. Tumor size as well as the body weight of mice was recorded during the experiment while tumor volume for each group was calculated according to the formula to monitor the growth of tumors. Finally, tumors were taken out, photographed and weighed at the 19th day. The change of tumor volumes (Figure 6A) showed that tumors in normal saline group grew fastest during the experimental process. However, tumors in mice treated with HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 were always the smallest in five groups. The final tumor morphology (Figure 6B) and tumor weights (Figure 6C) at 19th day further verified that tumors in normal saline group were the largest. The growth of tumors was all inhibited in the rest four treatment groups at different levels. In particular, the growth of tumors in mice of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group and HA/Ara$\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ group was significantly suppressed. The tumor inhibition ratio (Figure 6C) of Ara was low $(29.7\%)$ using normal saline group as control because of its low accumulation level in tumors and instability in blood circulation. The tumor inhibition ratio of IR820 was $47.6\%$ , indicating that breast cancer could be partly inhibited through photothermal therapy. By comparison, high tumor inhibition ratios of Ara- $\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8 $(72.9\%)$ and $H\mathrm{A}/\mathrm{Ara}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{IR}820@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ $(89.0\%)$ were found. Such high tumor inhibition ratios could be attributed to their high tumor-targeted ability, the well protection of prodrugs in blood circulation by ZIF-8 and the combination effect of PTT with chemotherapy. The antitumor ability of $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm{Ara–IR}820@\varpi$ ZIF-8 was higher than that of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, which could be due to its active tumor-targeted accumulation resulting from HA besides passive targeting. Weight change of mice displayed in Figure 6D was an important indicator to evaluate the safety of formulations. The body weights of mice decreased after the treatment with IR820 or Ara, indicating their higher side toxicity resulted from their nonspecific distribution. However, the body weights of mice treated with Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 or $\mathrm{HA}/$ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 were increasing slowly similar to the normal saline group. This could be explained as follows. Both the targeting drug delivery and pH-responsive release made AraIR820 accumulate into cancer cells selectively, avoiding the side effects to normal tissues. Therefore, the high tumor inhibition ratio and low side toxicity demonstrated our HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 could be used as an efficient chemo-photothermal therapy nanoplatform for tumors.
To further evaluate the histopathological toxicity of HA/Ara$\mathrm{IR}820@$ ZIF-8, tumors and main organs isolated from mice treated with different formulations were examined utilizing the classical hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining at the end of treatment. It could be seen from Figure 6E that no obvious impairment appeared in the main organs (spleen, heart, kidney, liver, and lung) for all groups. However, the pathologic features of tumors for treatment groups were quite different from that of normal saline group. Cells of normal morphology could be observed at the tumor tissues in normal saline group because it was basically ineffective for the growth of tumors. However, tumors in other groups showed apoptosis in different degrees as a result of the therapeutic effect, in which cells were irregularly shaped and fragmentized with weakened nuclear staining. Especially for HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 group, it was very difficult to find the intact cellular contour and the  nucleuses. In addition, both vast areas of cavities and lots of overflowed cytoplasm clearly implied the tumor necrosis, reflecting excellent tumor-killing ability of HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8. Thus, our newly constructed HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 could be applied as a safe drug delivery system for efficient chemo-photothermal therapy with low side effect.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we proposed a versatile prodrug strategy for encapsulating drugs in ZIF-8 (MOF) carrier based on the flexible chemistry modification. Up to date, only limited drugs with unique functional groups could be encapsulated satisfactorily in ZIF-8 by strong interactions. Our strategy made it possible for MOFs to load and deliver the general drugs without functional groups to interact strongly with ZIF-8. The drug Ara was chosen as the model drug since it could not be loaded in ZIF-8 with satisfactory drug loading content by itself. IR820 was utilized to bond with Ara for the formation of prodrug (AraIR820), in which sulfonic groups could strengthen the interaction between prodrug and ZIF-8. The HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 with active targeting capacity was thus innovatively designed and synthesized to overcome the limitation of MOFs on drug loading by the dragging effect from the functional groups in prodrug. Moreover, the IR820 endowed our prodrug with unique fluorescence imaging-guided photothermal therapy. The in vitro and in vivo results demonstrated that our nanoparticles performed excellent fluorescence imaging-guided chemophotothermal therapy against cancers with tumor-targeted and pH-responsive release behavior. Our strategy provides a robust route to solve the drug loading problems encountered by MOFs, enlarging the application of MOFs as universal nanocarriers for common drugs.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Ara $(>99.0\%)$ and HA $(>95.0\%$ , molecular weight $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ 10 000 DA) were supplied by Dalian Meilun Biotech Co., Ltd. IR820 $(80\%)$ was from Sigma-Aldrich. Triethylamine and 6-aminocaproic acid were supplied by Aladdin Industrial Corporation. Ethyl chloroformate was bought from Chengdu Huaxia Reagent Chemical Co., Ltd. Anhydrous N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF), $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ , and 2-methylimidazole (2-mim) were purchased from Beijing J & K Technology Co., Ltd. Deionized water was utilized in the experiment. All reagents were used as received.
Synthesis and Characterization of Ara-IR820: First, carboxylated IR820 (IR820-COOH) was synthesized based on published article.[31] IR820 was dissolved in anhydrous DMF, followed by the addition of 6-aminocaproic acid and triethylamine. The reaction solution was stirred at $85^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under nitrogen atmosphere for $4\ h$ . After that, its color was clearly changed from green into blue. DMF was removed and silica gel column chromatography was employed for the purification of IR820-COOH.
Then, Ara-IR820 was synthesized via the amide reaction between the amino groups of Ara and carboxyl groups in IR820-COOH.[32] Purified IR820-COOH was dissolved in anhydrous DMF and catalyzed by triethylamine with ethyl chloroformate in  ice bath under nitrogen atmosphere for 20 min. Subsequently, the DMF solution of Ara was added into the above reacting solution and the condition was changed into room temperature. Thin-layer chromatography was used to monitor the reaction process. After $72\mathrm{~h~}$ , the reaction solvent was removed and silica gel column chromatography was employed for the purification of Ara-IR820.
After above reactions, the structures of intermediates and reaction products were analyzed utilizing the $\mathsf{\Pi}^{\mathsf{I}}\mathsf{H}$ NMR (Bruker Avance 400 spectrometer). Dimethyl sulfoxide- $\cdot d_{6}$ $(\mathsf{D M S O-}d_{6})$ was used as solvent and tetramethylsilane was used  as the internal standard. ESI-MS (AB SCIEX API 4000) was also applied to characterize the samples.
Preparation of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8: Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was constructed according to the similar process reported in our previous work.[33] Typically, the solution of Ara-IR820 was prepared utilizing deionized water and added drop-wise into the aqueous solution of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O$ under stirring. After being stirred for $5\min$ , the mixed solution was drop-wise added into the 2-methylimidazole aqueous solution under vigorous stirring. The reaction solution was centrifuged after $10\min$ , and the obtained blue precipitate was washed with water for three times.
The prepared Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was sufficiently dispersed into the HA solution by ultrasonic. After the stirring of $48\mathrm{~h~}$ , blue precipitate was collected by centrifuge and washed thoroughly with water. Part of the synthesized HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was dispersed in water for the TEM characterization and DLS measurement. The other part of HA/AraIR820@ZIF-8 precipitate was dried in vacuum for other examinations.
Characterization: The morphology of $\mathsf{A r a}.|\mathsf{R8}20@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ and HA/Ara$1{\sf R}820@\sf Z{\sf I}F{\cdot}8$ was characterized utilizing the TEM (JEM-200CX). DLS (Brookhaven BI-200SM) was carried out to study the particle size and size distribution of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 and HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8. XRD (X'Pert3 Powder) equipped with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation was used to verify the specific crystal structure of ZIF-8 within the 2θ range of $5^{\circ}-50^{\circ}$ . The drug loading content of $H_{A/\mathsf{A r a-}}|\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ was calculated based on a UV–vis spectrophotometer (UV-8000S, Shanghai Metash Instruments Co., Ltd).
Photothermal Capability In Vitro: First of all, $H_{A/\mathsf{A r a-}}|\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ , Ara- $118820@$ ZIF-8, Ara-IR820, and IR820 aqueous solution $(7m L)$ were placed in a 12-pore plate separately to compare the photothermal effects of different formulations. The calculated concentration of IR820 in each group was $118\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{m}$ . The temperatures of water and ZIF-8 solution were also studied. IR820 solution received the laser irradiation of $\mathsf{1}\:\mathsf{W}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at $808~\mathsf{n m}$ for 5 min, while the wavelength of laser for other groups was changed into $660\ \mathsf{n m}$ based on the maximum absorbance from UV–vis spectra. Temperatures were recorded per $30\mathrm{~s~}$ At the end of these operations, thermal imager (Testo 869) was used to get thermographic images of these groups.
After that, the photothermal conversion abilities of HA/Ara-IR820@ ZIF-8 solution containing different concentrations of IR820 (118, 59, 23.6, 11.8, and $0\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~m~}$ ) were tested to display its concentration dependent effect.
In Vitro Release from $H A/A r a.I R8.20@\varTheta Z I F.8.$ The simulative release process of $H_{A/\mathsf{A r a-}}|\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ in vitro was carried out in a typical method.[34] PBS with different $\mathsf{p H}$ values $(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ =\ 5.0$ and 7.4) was prepared to verify the pH-responsive release behavior of HA/Ara- $118820@$ ZIF-8. Briefly, HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was fetched accurately and put into the PBS of different pH values. The release medium was then kept at $(37.0\pm0.5^{\cdot}$ ) $^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ and stirred at the rate of 100  rpm. At each scheduled time point, $3m L$ of release solvent was taken out by centrifugation and replaced with the equal volume of fresh PBS. UV–vis spectrophotometer was utilized to detect the concentrations of drugs in the release medium with different $\mathsf{p H}$ , and the accumulative release amount at each time point was calculated accordingly. These operations were repeated for three times.
Cellular Uptake  Assessment of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8: 4T1 cells were chosen to evaluate the cellular uptake behavior and antitumor ability of the prepared HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, which were incubated in the RPMI1640 culture medium.
Cellular uptake ability of 4T1 cells for HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 was studied qualitatively and quantitatively. As for qualitative evaluation, 4T1 cells were seeded into a six-well microplate and incubated for one day. After that, culture medium was replaced by the medium containing HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, or IR820. To verify the HA-mediated active targeting ability, competitive inhibition experiment was done based on the procedure in literature.[35] For this purpose, the cells were incubated with culture medium containing HA $(5\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}})$ for $2\ h$ before the addition of $H\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{A}r a.1\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}1\mathsf{F}.8$ . The concentration of IR820 at each group was fixed at $20\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ and the incubation time of cells in medium containing drugs was $4h$ . Then, cells were washed with fresh PBS thoroughly and observed under a fluorescent inverted microscope (ECLIPSE-Ti, Nikon). As for the quantitative assessment, cells were washed with fresh PBS thoroughly and treated with trypsin after the incubation of $4\ h$ based on the procedure in the literature.[36] The results were obtained by flow cytometry (BD FACSAria III).
Cytotoxicity Assay: The in vitro antitumor ability of HA/Ara$1\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ derived from the chemotherapy and PTT was learned through a cytotoxicity assay using the 3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2, 5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT) method.[37] The laser-assisted cell inhibition rates of Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820, the mixture of Ara and IR820, IR820, Ara, and laser irradiation alone were also tested for comparison. 4T1 cells were seeded into a 96-well microplate with the count of $5\times10^{3}$ per well and incubated for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, the culture medium was substituted with medium containing different formulations. The concentrations of IR820 or Ara varied from 0.5 to ${\mathsf{7}}0\times{\mathsf{10^{-6}}}\ {\mathsf{1}}$ m for each group (0.5, 1, 2, 5, and $10\times10^{-6}\mathrm{~M~}$ ). Cells were incubated at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $4\ h$ , then, cells were moved into the medium without drugs and received the laser irradiation of 1 W $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ for 1 min. The wavelength of laser was $808\ n m$ for the IR820 group as well as the mixture of Ara and IR820 group, while it was $660\ \mathsf{n m}$ for other groups based on the maximum absorbance from UV–vis spectra. After additional incubation of $20\mathsf{h}$ , $70\upmu\upiota$ PBS of MTT with the concentration of $5~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ was added into each well, and cells were incubated for another $4\ h$ . Finally, the supernatant was replaced by $150~\upmu\upiota$ of DMSO to dissolve the formazan. The absorbance (A) was measured by a microplate reader (ELIASA of PerkinElmer) at the wavelength of $490\ \mathsf{n m}$ .
The cell inhibition rates were calculated using the following formula[38] and the inhibition rate of each formulation on 4T1 cells was tested for three times
$$
(\%)=\frac{A_{\sf p o s i t i v e}-A_{\sf s a m p l e}}{A_{\sf p o s i t i v e}-A_{\sf b l a n k}}\times100\%
$$
where, the cells in positive group were treated with no drug, while the blank group just contained culture medium. The concentrations of formulations to realize the cell inhibition rates of $50\%$ ( $1mathsf{C}_{50}$ value) were then calculated.[39]
Hemolysis Test: The hemolysis ratio of $H_{A/\mathsf{A r a-}}|\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ was tested on red blood cells (RBCs) extracted from the  plasma of a New Zealand white rabbit. RBCs were washed with normal saline thoroughly and dispersed in normal saline to prepare the $2\%$ RBCs suspension. HA/Ara-IR820 $@$ ZIF-8 $(0.75~\mathsf{m L})$ ) of a series of concentrations (10, 20, 50, 100, 200, and $500~\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1};$ ) was mixed with $2\%$ RBCs suspension $(7.25~\mathsf{m L})$ and $1.70~\mathsf{m L}$ of saline. The above mixture was incubated at the temperature of $37.0\pm1.0^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After incubation, the mixture was centrifuged at 1500  rpm for $15\mathrm{\min}$ , and then the absorbance of supernatant was measured using a UV–vis spectrophotometer at $540\ \mathsf{n m}$ .[40] As for the positive group, the hemolysis ratio of water was tested by the mixture of $2\%$ RBCs suspension and $1.25~\mathsf{m L}$ of water. The hemolysis ratio of normal saline was acquired by the mixture of $2\%$ RBCs suspension and normal saline $(7.25~\mathsf{m L})$ , which was the negative group. All the operations were performed for three times. The hemolysis ratios of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8 were calculated as follows[41]
$$
(\%)=\frac{A_{\sf s a m p l e}-A_{\sf n e g a t i v e}}{A_{\sf p o s i t i v e}-A_{\sf n e g a t i v e}}\times100\%
$$
Establishment of Tumor-Bearing Mice Models: Female balb/c mice (6–8 weeks old) were provided by the laboratory animal center of
Shandong University (China). All mice had free access to water and conventional feed during the experiment. The operations followed the requests in the Shandong University Animal Experiment Ethics Review and the Health Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals of National Institutes.
4T1 cells were dispersed in PBS with the density of $2.0\times10^{7}$ cells $m L^{-1}$ . And then $0.1~\mathsf{m L}$ of cells was inoculated into mice at the armpit of right forelimb to establish the model of mouse mammary carcinoma. The largest diameter (L) and smallest diameter $(\mathsf{W})$ of each tumor were monitored after the inoculation to calculate the tumor volume[42]
$$
\mathsf{T u m o r v o l u m e}=\frac{L\times\mathsf{W}^{2}}{2}
$$
In Vivo/Ex Vivo NIRF Imaging and Biodistribution: As typical near infrared dyes, IR820 has the intrinsic fluorescence to be used for nearinfrared fluorescence imaging, which could be utilized to monitor the biodistribution of $H\mathsf{A}/\mathsf{A}\mathsf{r a}.1\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ . When the tumor volume reached about $\mathsf{l}50\mathsf{m}\mathsf{m}^{3}$ , mice were treated with HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, and IR820 via the tail intravenous injection. The dose of IR820 for each group was $2~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ . After being injected, mice were narcotized with chloral hydrate by intraperitoneal injection and taken photos using an in vivo imaging system (Xenogen IVIS Kinetic system) at 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, and $24\ h$ postinjection.
At the end of this experiment, mice were executed. The kidney, tumor, liver, spleen, heart, and lung were dissected and analyzed to further study the targeting distribution effect of HA/Ara-IR820@ZIF-8.
Measurements of Photothermal Effect In Vivo: When the 4T1 tumor volume reached about $150~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{m}^{3}$ , mice were injected with HA/Ara$118820@$ ZIF-8, Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, IR820, and normal saline. Followed that, mice were treated with near infrared laser of 1 W $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ for 5 min at $4\ h$ after the injection. Mice of IR820 group were irradiated with the laser of $808~\mathsf{n m}$ , and the laser used for other groups was $660\ \mathsf{n m}$ based on the maximum absorbance from UV–vis spectra. The infrared thermal images of mice were recorded during the irradiation process to compare the PTT effect of different formulations.
In Vivo Antitumor Effect Assessment: In vivo antitumor effect was one of the most critical factors to evaluate the  feasibility  of formulations, which was tested on the 4T1-bearing balb/c mice for our HA/Ara$1{\tt R}820@\mathbb{Z}1{\sf F}.8$ . Mice were randomly divided into five groups $(n=5$ ) when the tumor volumes reached $\mathsf{l}50\mathsf{m}\mathsf{m}^{3}$ and then treated with $\mathsf{H A}/$ Ara- $1{\tt R}820@\mathbb{Z}1{\sf F}.8$ , Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, IR820, Ara, and normal saline via tail intravenous injection. The injection was performed every three days (day 1, day 4, day 7, day 10, day 13, and day 16) and the dose of IR820 was $2~\mathsf{m g}\ \mathsf{k g}^{-1}$ . After each injection, mice in $H_{A/\mathsf{A}\mathsf{r a}-1\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}^{}|\mathsf{F}-8$ , Ara$1{\tt R}820@\mathbb{Z}1{\sf F}.8$ , and IR820 groups were irradiated with near infrared light for the PTT effect. The tumor volumes and body weights were measured every other day during the treatment. At the end, mice were executed to take out tumors for weight and taking photographs.
The tumor inhibition ratio based on tumor weight was calculated for each group to compare the antitumor effect intuitively using the following equation[43]
$$
(\%)=\frac{(\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{c}}-\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{t}})}{\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{c}}}\times100\%
$$
$\mathsf{W}_{\mathsf{c}}$ represented the average tumor weight for normal saline group, while $\mathsf{W}_{\mathrm{t}}$ represented the final tumor weight of treatment group.
Histopathological Study: After the in vivo antineoplastic experiment, mice administrated with $H_{A/\mathsf{A r a-}}|\mathsf{R}820@\mathsf{Z}|\mathsf{F}.8$ , Ara-IR820@ZIF-8, IR820, Ara, and normal saline were sacrificed. Tumor and major organs (spleen, heart, kidney, liver, and lung) were taken out for the histopathological study. These isolated organs were washed with saline and soaked in $4\%$ formaldehyde solution overnight. Then, the organs were embedded in paraffin, and sliced and dyed by hematoxylin and eosin for the observation of the pathological damage. Photographs were taken to evaluate the security of as-prepared HA/ Ara-IR820@ZIF-8.
